From: rjc@gnu.ai.mit.edu (Ray)
Subject: your mail

Aidan Willis writes:
> General extropianism
> A tenet of this list is that immortality is good.  One of the
> conclusions of work on the genetic algorithm is that increasing
> organism lifespan slows its effectiveness.  Surely we must each
> conclude that while immortality for oneself is fine it has to be
> prevented in the race? (Unless I suppose one wants to keep one's
> competition from improving...)

  This is a frequent misconception that nonimmortalists present as
argument in sci.life-extension all the time. The counter argument
is that human evolution has moved from the physical domain to the
information domain. Genetic algorithms do not support arguments
against human immortality -- in fact I could argue the reverse.
You don't destroy the computer everytime an algorithm's fitness reaches
a low point. In today's world, it's not how much you can bench
press which leads to success but how much you know and the fitness of
those ideas.

  A number of people argue against immortality because it halts
genetic evolution. But what is so good about genetic evolution?
Human beings have evolved more in the last 100 years than nature has
done in 4 billion. There is also no reason to believe that humans
will keep genetically evolving anyway.

