Date: Fri, 2 Jul 93 20:49:42 PDT
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: AIT VirtSem: Critical Bits, Structure, and Information

I think we're retracing old ground, so I'll be very brief on Hal
Finney's point:


> > And a "genius" program might look for even more clever compressions. That
> > is, much shorter descriptions.
> 
> I am still trying to improve my understanding of these subjects, too,
> so I certainly don't claim to have any final answers.  But I believe that
> AIT as Chaitin describes it does not include this notion of models dumb
> and smart.
> 
> Rather, the algorithmic information content of a string S is _defined_
> to be the length of the shortest self-delimiting program for calculating
> that string.  This definition does not refer to the smartness of the
> program.  It is perhaps true that we might choose to call shorter programs
> "smarter" than longer ones which output the same string, but that is not
> really part of the definition.

Sure, Chaitin doesn't use my terms "dumb" and "smart," especially as
he's writing formal papers and doing proofs and I'm just talking on a
mailing list (so I can be more informal).

Whenever we speak of "finding a shorter description," this is where
the "intelligence" point enters. When Chaitin speaks of finding short
programs (AIT length of some string or object), and not knowing for
sure if there isn't an even shorter program, the idea of *different*
programs and/or TMs has already been raised. This is clearly present in
several of his papers.

(So as not to start another circular argument, a la the arguments
about information, complexity, order, etc., the term "intelligence" is
oriented toward the cleverness of the compression algorithm, the
cleverness and power of the "program length" generator, and should not
be taken as having anything to do, per se, with animal intelligence.
Though of course it does, in some interesting senses.)


-Tim

