Date: Fri, 2 Jul 93 23:21:29 -0700
From: hfinney@shell.portal.com
Subject: AIT VirtSem: AIT & Physics

I've been thinking more about Nick's questions, and I have some thoughts
on the relationship between AIT and the issue of reversible/non-reversible
computations.

Chaitin defines H(S) to be the algorithmic information content of string
S, the size of the smallest program which produces S on an arbitrary universal
computer U.  He also defines H(S/T) to be the size of the smallest program
which produces S, given T as input.  The difference between H(S) and H(S/T)
depends on the degree to which T "helps" in the production of S.

In general, H(S) <= H(T) + H(S/T).  This states that if we have a program
to produce T, and a program to produce S given T, we therefore have a
program to produce S.  (All of the <= symbols here are approximate, given
the O(1) uncertainty in H due to different choices of U.)


In Bennett's formulation of reversible computing, certain operations are  
logically irreversible and discard information.  Those are the ones for which,
given the outputs, we can't uniquely determine the inputs.  Addition is
a simple example.

In general, irreversible operations can be made reversible by adding
some extra outputs to disambiguate the possible inputs.  While a = x+y is
irreversible, (a,b) = (x+y,x-y) is reversible.  Given a and b one can
deduce what x and y are.

So, a calculation which is irreversible can be run in a reversible way
simply by saving enough information during the calculation to allow running
the algorithm backwards.  If we are taking X as input and producing Y,
this may mean that we also have to produce extra bits Z to allow this
reversal.

Applying the AIT formula above to this case where we take in X and produce
output YZ, we know that H(YZ) <= H(X) + H(YZ/X).   

However, the one extra thing that we know, because the computation is 
reversible, is that we can take YZ as input and produce X using the very
same program, by running it backwards.  That means that H(X/YZ) <= H(YZ/X).
(The "<=" is because we know that a program of size H(YZ/X) will allow us
to produce X given YZ, but there could be a smaller one that would work as
well.) More generally, any reversible program with input A and output B
must have that H(A/B) <= H(B/A).  But also H(B/A) <= H(A/B), by symmetry. 
Therefore H(A/B) = H(B/A) for all strings A and B which are the input and
output of a reversible computer program.  (This is my main result.)

For the case at hand, then, H(YZ/X) = H(X/YZ).  Define XYZ* to be the size 
of this program, and we have:

H(YZ) <= H(X) + XYZ*  and also H(X) <= H(YZ) + XYZ*.  Thus, to within the
precision of XYZ*, H(YZ) and H(X) are the same size.

In the cases we are considering in discussing Go or evolution, XYZ* is
perhaps relatively small.  It consists of the rules of the program which
evolves the competing members.  I argued that in the case of Go it should
be a few hundred Kbits at most.  Perhaps the same thing could be true of
evolution, given the simplicity of GP codes.  Compared to the sizes of the
genomes of complex creatures, or of Go programs which could beat humans,
the evolution programs may be much smaller.

This would imply that a reversible evolutionary process would maintain the
AI content approximately constant.

An irreversible program, OTOH, would have different effects.  It would
no longer be true that H(A/B) = H(B/A), since a program which given A could
produce B would not generally suffice to produce A given B.  (After all,
it could simply discard all of A and produce some small number B, so we
could hardly expect it to be able to go in the opposite direction.)

If a "relatively small" evolution program of size XY* operates on X to
produce Y, we have that H(Y) <= H(X) + XY*, so if XY* is small then we
have, approximately, H(Y) <= H(X).

Thus, logically irreversible evolutionary processes can decrease the AI
content (compared to logically reversible ones, which always keep it
constant).

Of course, these results only point up the inadequacy of H as a measure
of information content, since evidently at best we can "break even"
and at worst we lose information.  This should sound familiar; it is
simply the second law of thermodynamics in AIT dressing.  H is analogous
(if not identical?) to the negentropy of the system, and can only
decrease.

I think the next step would be to try to analyze this process in terms
of logical depth.  I need to try to find a copy of the Herken book so I
can understand better what Bennett is getting at.  Robin asked about
the degree to which logical depth has a unique definition, and I'm not
sure how the degree of arbitrariness in logical depth compares to that
in AI.

We could ask, for "most" strings S, what can we say about the members of
the set?  We know that only 1/256 of all S's have an an AI which is smaller
than S by 8 bits.  What about the LD for such S's?  Is there a limit on how
much computing time it will take to compress most of these by a factor of
256? 

With a better picture of how logical depth works, it may be possible to 
discuss the circumstances under which evolution-style processes can or
will increase the logical depth of objects.

Hal

