Date: Fri, 2 Jul 93 23:19:24 -0700
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: AIT VirtSem: Does a String Have an Intrinsic AIT Value?

Hal Finney has more than once expressed dissatisfaction with the abstract
nature of AIT, with the inability of AIT to actually "find" the minimal
program length (= description) of a given string.

In other posts, I have argued that the idea that there is an intrinsic AIT
value is false, that the whole idea of AIT is that there may be various
shorter descriptions of a string, as generated by various "clever"
programs, but that in general we cannot mechanize, or formalize the process
of finding these shorter descriptions.

This problem, of finding shorter (more concise forms, "laws") descriptions,
is common in many fields:

- cryptography and cryptanalysis...analyzing an apparently random-looking
string and finding the "trick" that makes a plaintext message fall out. (On
older ciphers, by exploiting the redundancy of English, the non-maximal
entropy, to deduce a "shorter description.").

- crystallography and molecule analysis...deducing the structure, the much
shorter description, from a mass of random-looking data (the double helix
of DNA being a classic example of a "genius" program, in the language I've
used and which Hal dislikes).

- scene and picture analysis, when noisy images or incomplete data sets are
converted to "probable" patterns.

- science in general is the process of applying intelligence to masses of
seeming random data...positions of planets, chemical properties of the
elements, and so on. Induction, Occam's Razor, and the "unreasonable
effectiveness of mathematics" are intimately related to AIT. 

- an extraterrestrial civilization sends out a repeating sequence of bits.
If it repeats, we have reason to believe the string is not "noise," is not
fully random, and that bits of information must be carried in the pattern.
Finding a shorter description is thus closely related to decoding this
alien message.


Let me illustrate these points with a bit string example:


100010001010010010001010100000111000111111111100111000001010100010010
010100010001


This bit string can be "described" or given as the output of a computer
program which is, say, "PRINT
'10001000101001001000101010000011100011111111110011100000101010001001001
0100010001'"

This propram is not very concise...the description is as long as the string
itself. It turns out that nearly all "random strings" are like this, that
is, they can't be described with significantly fewer bits than they already
are.

But does a shorter description exist? One can see that there are more 0s
than 1s, implying the string is not "normal" (in the Borel sense, a normal
number has the same number of digits in every base....a truly random number
is normal in this sense).

Alice and Bob set out to find a shorter, more concise description of this
string.

Alice looks at this and decides to use her various compression programs on
it. She runs the Lempel-Ziv algorithms (common in most compression
routines) and gets a bit string that is only 73.2 % the size of the
original. (She runs it again, and gets no further decrease in size...in
fact, the double-compressed string is *not* shorter--it is slightly larger
than the once-compressed string. She tries it a third time and sees much
the same result, so she proudly declares her compression (once) to be the
"shortest description." She declares the string to have an algorithmic
information content of 0.73 x 81 bits = 59 bits.

(Technically, the bits used to describe the L-Z compression itself are part
of the description. For long strings, or for many of these, this length can
be amortized over many bits and is not a major contributor to the AIT
length. A minor detail.)

Is a shorter description possible? 

Of course it is "possible." Can it be found? How?

Chaitin's results tell us we'll never know that a shorter description isn't
possible...and that no mechanized approach is possible that guarantees
we'll find it. (The Halting Problem.)

But Bob is not deterred--Chaitin's results give him hope that human or
machine cleverness can succeed where Lempel-Ziv failed, and where simple
mechanical methods fail.

He crunches, he draws pictures...then he notices the strange distributions
of run lengths (which Lempel-Ziv used, but only "dumbly"). In particular,
he thinks of crystal diffraction patterns.

He thinks of arrays, new ways to rearrange the string.

He also notes (duh!) that the string has 81 bits in it. So he thinks of a 9
x 9 square and he arranges the bit string as follows:


100010001
010010010
001010100
000111000
111111111
100111000
001010100
010010010
100010001

Now he sees the "1"s running on the diagonals and on the bisectors....a star.

He now has a very simple description: "9 x 9, star"


This takes fewer than the 59 bits of Alice's solution, so he wins.

But he's left with the nagging idea that an even more concise description
exists, one he may never find. Chaitin says he can never prove that his
solution is in fact the shortest one.

(Technical note again: in a toy system with no concept of a "star," or of
what a 9 x 9 array is, it is quite possible that this new solution would be
"incomprehensible" to the formal system and more total bits would be needed
to explain everything. This is the "context/embedding" problem again. As in
this easy-to-understand example, which actually reflects how a real "proof"
would work, this is sufficient. I will grant that there are real issues
here, just as there are when we say the info content of DNA is misleading,
because embryology depends on knowledge scattered around in the "context"
and the "embedding.")

------

Now this simple example is actually quite illustrative of how hard
technical problems are often solved by sudden insights, sudden recastings
of problems in new ways, rearrangements that make the problem suddenly
appear simple.

This is the beauty of algorithmic information theory, and this is why it
has so many points of contact with important problems to Extropians.

I hope this example helps.

-Tim May

